    "body": "View of the Pecoy project area in Peru. Credit: Pecoy Copper.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 16, 2026\nNew drilling by Pecoy Copper (TSXV: PCU) at its namesake copper-gold project in southern Peru produced gold grades more than twice the existing resource average as drilling tests both the known deposit and potential extensions. Hole PEC26-082 cut 727.25 metres at 0.30% copper and 0.11 gram gold per tonne from 264 metres depth in the South Breccia area, Pecoy said Monday . Within that interval, 178 metres returned 0.30% copper and 0.20 gram gold from 474 metres, Pecoy said Monday.\n“We view these results as positive, with broad, higher-grade intervals across three separate areas supporting potential resource growth,” Scotia Capital mining analyst Eric Winmill said Monday in a note.\nThe higher gold grades could improve by-product credits in a future copper operation, although their value depends on recovery tests and economic studies. The main South Breccia interval lies inside the existing resource pit shell; separate drilling beneath the shell provides the clearer test of additional scale.\nNext tests\nIn the Northwest area, hole PEC26-081 cut 220 metres at 0.40% copper and 0.030% molybdenum from 104 metres depth. A separate, deeper interval cut 317.5 metres at 0.22% copper from 691.5 metres, extending beneath the resource pit shell and remaining mineralized to the end of the hole. The hole tested the edge of MTP-01, a geophysical target. Results from two holes drilled closer to its interpreted core are among the next tests of the expansion case, Desjardins Capital Markets mining analyst Bryce Adams said. The East Breccia also returned 236 metres at 0.40% copper from 174 metres depth. Adams highlighted both the copper results and the possibility that molybdenum and gold could add value alongside the principal metal.\nAn April 2025 estimate put inferred resources at 865 million tonnes at 0.34% copper, 0.012% molybdenum, 0.05 gram gold and 1.33 grams silver per tonne, containing about 6.45 billion lb. of copper. The resource is constrained by a conceptual open-pit shell and has not demonstrated economic viability.\nResource update\nThe project lies about 275 km northwest of Arequipa, at an elevation of about 1,650 metres. More than 26,000 metres of a planned 35,000-metre program have been drilled, with assays pending from seven completed holes and four holes in progress, Adams said. He put cash at about $80 million following a financing with two strategic investors. Metallurgical work is assessing improved gold recovery and production of a separate molybdenum concentrate. Adams expects an updated resource in early 2027 and a preliminary economic assessment next year. He rates the shares buy with a $4 target, classifying the investment as speculative.\nPecoy shares fell 1.1% to $1.83 Monday morning in Toronto, valuing the company at about $386 million (US$270 million). The stock has ranged between 85¢ and $2.29 over the past year.\nPrint\nTAG: copper copper exploration gold gold exploration gold projects\nCOMMODITY: Copper Gold\nREGION: Canada South America\nCOMPANY: Pecoy Copper\nRepublish this article",
